From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: decode field-coded MPEG-2

A field-coded MPEG-2 stream carries each field as its own picture, and cedrus
refused it outright: no output at all, and the VE interrupt counter never moved.

Two things were wrong, and both had to be fixed to get a picture.

The pair of fields has to land in ONE capture buffer, which means holding that
buffer across the first field's job -- V4L2_BUF_FLAG_M2M_HOLD_CAPTURE_BUF.  The
capability was granted to H.264 and HEVC and explicitly cleared for everything
else, so the client could not say "there is more to come" and its QBUF was
refused before the driver ever saw a frame.  Confirmed per format on an H713
before the change: MG2S and VP8F reported REQBUFS capabilities 0x1d, S264 and
S265 0x3d.

With that granted the stream decoded, at one interrupt per coded field, but the
last macroblock row of every field was wrong -- a fixed cluster at the bottom
right, present even in an intra picture with no prediction history.  The cause is
PICCODEDSIZE: its height field is macroblock ROWS, and a field picture has half
of them.  The engine was told 36 rows where the field carried 18 and ran past the
end of its data.

Measured against ffmpeg on the same elementary stream, luma PSNR over 31 frames
rises from 35.73 dB to 63.70 dB, and the intra frame's six bad macroblocks go to
none.  The per-frame profile is then a clean GOP sawtooth -- 69 dB at each
I-field decaying to 62 by the end of the GOP and resetting -- which is what
MPEG-2's IDCT accuracy tolerance looks like on a conformant decoder.  The single
remaining outlier is the last frame at 24 dB, whose bad macroblocks start at
(0, 22): exactly where ffmpeg reports "ac-tex damaged at 0 22" in this sample.
That frame is the stream's own damage, not the decoder's.

Frame-picture MPEG-2 is byte-identical before and after, progressive and
interlaced alike, because the new height applies only when picture_structure is
not FRAME.  VP8 and the H.264/HEVC gates are unchanged and v4l2-compliance stays
49/49.

Tested with samples.ffmpeg.org/MPEG2/mpeg2_field_encoding.ts, which is genuinely
field-coded: 31 top-field plus 31 bottom-field pictures and no frame pictures.
Neither ffmpeg's mpeg2video encoder nor mjpegtools' mpeg2enc can produce such a
stream locally -- the former only emits frame pictures and the latter segfaults
in MacroBlock::FieldME().

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

Origin: https://github.com/cstenger/allwinner-h713-mainline/blob/43b2cdd4aa1b161a281db8b10f02ce70085e2d42/patches/kernel/0123-media-cedrus-decode-field-coded-mpeg2.patch (0123-media-cedrus-decode-field-coded-mpeg2.patch, commit 0f03d0b)
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
@@ -658,7 +658,18 @@
 
 	vq = v4l2_m2m_get_vq(ctx->fh.m2m_ctx, V4L2_BUF_TYPE_VIDEO_OUTPUT);
 
+	/*
+	 * Formats that can decode two coded pictures into one capture buffer,
+	 * which needs that buffer held across the first one's job.
+	 *
+	 * MPEG-2 is here for FIELD pictures: a field-coded stream carries each
+	 * field as its own picture, and the pair has to be interleaved into a
+	 * single frame. Without this the decoder cannot say "there is more to
+	 * come", the client's QBUF is refused, and a field-coded stream does
+	 * not decode at all.
+	 */
 	switch (ctx->src_fmt.pixelformat) {
+	case V4L2_PIX_FMT_MPEG2_SLICE:
 	case V4L2_PIX_FMT_H264_SLICE:
 	case V4L2_PIX_FMT_HEVC_SLICE:
 		vq->subsystem_flags |=
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_mpeg2.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_mpeg2.c
@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@
 	struct cedrus_dev *dev = ctx->dev;
 	struct vb2_queue *vq;
 	const u8 *matrix;
+	unsigned int coded_height;
 	unsigned int i;
 	u32 reg;
 
@@ -108,8 +109,17 @@
 
 	/* Set frame dimensions. */
 
+	/*
+	 * A FIELD picture codes half the frame's lines, so it has half the
+	 * macroblock rows. Handing the engine the frame height makes it run
+	 * past the end of the field's data.
+	 */
+	coded_height = seq->vertical_size;
+	if (pic->picture_structure != V4L2_MPEG2_PIC_FRAME)
+		coded_height /= 2;
+
 	reg = VE_DEC_MPEG_PICCODEDSIZE_WIDTH(seq->horizontal_size);
-	reg |= VE_DEC_MPEG_PICCODEDSIZE_HEIGHT(seq->vertical_size);
+	reg |= VE_DEC_MPEG_PICCODEDSIZE_HEIGHT(coded_height);
 
 	cedrus_write(dev, VE_DEC_MPEG_PICCODEDSIZE, reg);
 
